  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nFor today, a weather system continues to move further south and\neast for a dry day in store across eastern New York and western\nNew England. For Saturday afternoon and evening, an upper level\nshortwave is supported by latest forecast model guidances to \nmove through eastern New York and western New England for one \nmore day of unsettled weather conditions. While most locations \nwill see partly to mostly cloudy skies, there are medium chances\n(40-60%) for rain showers and a rumble of thunder for southern \nVermont and the Berkshires during the afternoon hours Saturday. \nThere are low chances (less than 30 percent) across the southern\nAdirondacks, Mohawk Valley, Schoharie Valley, Greater Capital \nDistrict, and Lake George- Saratoga regions. With the nature of \nthe rain showers to be on the light side, there are no flooding \nconcerns for Saturday afternoon and evening. Then, surface high \npressure builds in from the northwest and dry conditions return \nfor the rest of the Labor Day weekend across eastern New York \nand western New England through Tuesday next week.\n\nWednesday night into Thursday next week is looking to be our \nnext best chance (50-75%) for precipitation across eastern New \nYork and western New England. Exact rainfall amounts and the \ntrack of the weather system has been fluctuating with latest \nensemble forecast model guidances, so forecast confidence is\nlow at this time on potential impacts. With dry conditions \nbefore our next round of precipitation, flooding concerns are \nvery low at this forecast time.",
